  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates for hypothyroidism are present. PGS005218 is the best-supported direct-match overall because it is explicitly disease-focused for hypothyroidism (publication: “Genome-wide association study and polygenic risk prediction of hypothyroidism.”), targets an incident hypothyroidism endpoint, uses a strong genome-wide shrinkage method (PRS-CS) with ~1.11M variants, and reports a high validation AUROC (0.859) with a very large stated validation sample (n=441,692) in Europeans. The main limitation is that the reported AUROC appears to be a full-model metric with covariates that include thyroid biomarkers (TSH, T4, anti-TPO), which the provided domain guidance flags as near-outcome covariates that can inflate AUROC and are not PRS-comparable; the context does not provide PRS-only AUROC/R² or an incremental metric isolating the PRS contribution, preventing a high-confidence selection over other endpoint-faithful alternatives."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported aligns to bladder cancer and trait_efo is urinary bladder carcinoma/cancer; phenotyping_reported is bladder cancer/incident bladder cancer/urothelial carcinoma). No candidate provides clearly PRS-only AUROC/R2; most AUROCs shown are full-model (often with age/sex/smoking covariates) and thus are not directly comparable as PRS discrimination metrics per the provided domain guidance, which limits certainty.

Among the set, PGS000611 (GWAS Hits, 13 variants; UKB development; EUR evaluation) has relatively strong, PRS-relevant effect-size evidence (OR per SD 1.284, 95% CI 1.211–1.361) plus supportive tail discrimination (top 1% vs rest OR 2.66, 95% CI 1.79–3.93) and a moderate full-model AUROC (0.567). This combination is more informative than candidates whose apparent advantage is primarily very high full-model AUROC driven by extensive non-genetic covariates (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782 with full-model AUROC ~0.80 and smoking/BMI covariates) or candidates with weaker OR/AUROC signals (e.g., several PRS-CS/lassosum/P+T scores with AUROC ~0.52–0.54 and OR ~1.08–1.14).

Main limitation: multiple near-clone Cancer PRSweb “GWAS Hits/P+T” models (PGS000610/611/612/613) show very similar endpoints and full-model AUROC (~0.567–0.572) with overlapping ORs (~1.28–1.30), so the best choice is not uniquely determined from the visible evidence; PGS000611 is selected because it has the clearest additional tail-risk metric (top 1% OR) alongside strong per-SD OR."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS004861","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 8 candidates are direct matches to dilated cardiomyopathy by trait_efo, with endpoint wording differences (general DCM vs non-ischemic DCM vs clinical DCM) that introduce some phenotype-alignment ambiguity. No candidate reports clearly PRS-only (covariate-free) AUROC/R²; the visible AUROC values are full-model or univariate and are not directly comparable across differing covariate sets and endpoints per the provided domain guidance, lowering certainty.

PGS004861 is preferred because it is a straightforward, endpoint-faithful ‘Dilated cardiomyopathy’ PRS (not MTAG-labeled), evaluated in EUR with basic covariates (age, age^2, sex, PC1-10) and large validation (n=347,585), avoiding the stronger subtype/endpoint shifts seen in the ‘non-ischemic’ and ‘clinical’ variants. Its effect size (OR=1.56) is in the ‘strong’ range (≥1.5) per the domain guidance, supporting meaningful PRS signal even without PRS-only discrimination metrics.

Main limitation: several sibling models show similar or higher effect sizes and/or reported metrics (e.g., MTAG versions and non-ischemic/clinical endpoints), but differences in endpoint definition, evaluation ancestry context, and metric comparability prevent a clearly best choice from the visible evidence alone."}

